  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-40515",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nLet's denote the elements of $U,W,$ and $V$ respectively as \n\n$\\,$ $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\},\\,$ $\\{Q({v}_{1}),Q({v}_{2}),Q({v}_{3})\\},\\,$ and $\\,\\{Q({w}_{1}),Q({w}_{2})\\}.$\n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})=0.$\n\n- $Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2})=0,\\,Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0,\\,Q({v}_{2})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0.$\n\n- $Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2}) & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=20-3=17\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not and orthogonal set.\n\n- For the Set $V,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2}) & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]=3-4+1=0,\\,✓ \\\\ Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{3}) & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=2+8+18=28\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2}) & ={w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]=-15+15=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$W$ only\".",
